    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm running into some issues with my levain stability. The pH levels are swinging way too much between feedings—sometimes it's super acidic and then it levels out too fast, which is messing with my dough's hydration and rise. I'm wondering how much the ambient temp in my kitchen vs the protein content of my rye/AP mix is contributing to this microbial volatility? Just trying to stabilize the culture.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a classic case of your kitchen acting like a chaotic, temperature-shifting oven for a very sensitive, slightly mischievous colony of yeast and bacteria. The pH swings you're seeing are likely the result of your rye and AP flour acting as a volatile buffet for the microbes, while your ambient temperature is just the erratic conductor directing the chaos.\n\nThink of your levain as a tiny, bustling underground city where the temperature is the mood and the flour is the food supply. If your kitchen temp is fluctuating, it's like the city's power grid is flickering on and off, causing the yeast to panic and the bacteria to get confused about whether to start fermenting or just hang out.\n\nHere's the thing: Rye flour is full of proteins and complex sugars that are basically a buffet for lactic acid bacteria. When the temperature drops even a little bit, these bacteria go into a \"hibernation\" mode, but when the temp spikes, they wake up and start churning out acid like it's their job. This is why your pH swings so wildly; the bacteria are reacting to the heat changes just like a goblin reacts to a sudden drop in the sun.\n\nYou can't really blame the",
      "source": "target",
