    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what do you mean by \"just like a goblin reacts to a sudden drop in the sun\"? I'm not really following that analogy. Can we stick to the chemistry? Specifically, how does the higher enzymatic activity in rye versus AP flour affect the buffering capacity of the starter during those temperature spikes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right to ask for the chemistry, though I must admit, the goblin analogy was just a quick mental shortcut to explain why the bacteria are so sensitive to your kitchen's mood swings. Rye flour is indeed a much more volatile environment than all-purpose flour because of its higher enzymatic activity and protein content, which act like a chaotic buffet for the microbes.\n\nWhen the temperature spikes, the enzymes in the rye become hyper-active, breaking down starches into sugars at a frantic pace. This sudden influx of food causes the lactic acid bacteria to feast and ferment rapidly, dumping out acid faster than the yeast can consume it or the flour's natural buffers can neutralize it. In contrast, AP flour has fewer active enzymes and a simpler protein structure, so it acts more like a stable, low-key tavern where the microbes don't get as excited about the temperature changes.\n\nThe buffering capacity of your starter is essentially how well the mixture can resist those pH swings. Rye's high enzymatic activity means it generates acid so quickly during a temperature spike that the buffer system gets overwhelmed, leading to a sharp drop in pH. Once the temp drops, the activity slows, and the pH might seem to level out, but the",
      "source": "target",
